 add('metric','Changing metric units','Metric units measure length, mass and capacity.~100 cm = 1 m; 1000 m = 1 km.~1000 g = 1 kg; 1000 ml = 1 litre.',
 'A smaller unit needs a larger number.~For area, square the length conversion: 1 m² = 10 000 cm².~For volume, cube it: 1 m³ = 1 000 000 cm³.',
 '2.4 m = 2.4 × 100 = 240 cm.~3.5 litres = 3500 ml.~2 m² = 2 × 10 000 = 20 000 cm².',
 'Convert 3.6 m into centimetres. Explain why 1 m² is 10 000 cm².',360,'3.6 × 100 = 360 cm.~A square metre is 100 cm by 100 cm.~100 × 100 = 10 000 cm².',
 'The area conversion is not the same as the length conversion.','Metric units=Units linked by powers of ten, such as metres and centimetres.~Capacity=How much a container can hold.',
 'How many centimetres are in 2.5 metres?','250.','25.','0.025.','Each metre contains 100 centimetres: 2.5 × 100 = 250.')
 add('missing-average','Finding a missing value from a mean','The mean shares the total equally.~Total = mean × number of values.~A missing value is the part of that total you do not know.',
 'Work out the required total.~Add the known values.~Subtract their total from the required total.',
 'Four values have a mean of 6, so their total is 24.~Three values are 3, 5 and 7: total 15.~The missing value is 24 − 15 = 9.',
 'Five numbers have a mean of 8. Four are 4, 6, 9 and 11. Find the fifth.',10,'The total must be 5 × 8 = 40.~The known values total 30.~The missing value is 10.',
 'The mean is not the total.','Total=The sum of all the values.~Missing value=A value that must be found using the information given.',
 'Three numbers have a mean of 7. What is their total?','21.','7.','10.','Total = 3 × 7 = 21.')
 add('bar-charts','Bar charts and pictograms','A bar chart compares categories.~Dual bars compare two groups for each category.~A composite bar stacks parts to show a total.',
 'Start with a labelled scale and equal-width bars.~Leave gaps between categories.~For a pictogram, give a key and use part-symbols accurately.',
 'A reading club has 6 Year 7 and 4 Year 8 pupils on Monday.~Dual bars show 6 and 4 separately.~A composite bar stacks 6 and 4 to a height of 10.',
 'A composite bar contains 8 walkers and 5 cyclists. What is its total height? Explain how a dual bar chart would show the data.',13,'8 + 5 = 13 pupils.~A dual chart has two separate bars for the category.~A key identifies walkers and cyclists.',
 'A stacked bar reaches the total, not the value of its final part.','Dual bar chart=Two bars for each category, comparing two groups.~Composite bar chart=A bar split into stacked parts that make a total.',
 'A bar stacks 7 girls and 9 boys. What is the total?','16.','9.','2.','The stacked parts add: 7 + 9 = 16.')
 add('time-series','Graphs showing change over time','A time series records values in time order.~Time goes on the horizontal axis.~The vertical axis shows the measured value and its unit.',
 'Choose even scales and plot each pair of values.~Join readings in time order when this makes sense.~Look for trends, repeating patterns and unusual points.',
 'A plant is 4 cm tall on day 0, 7 cm on day 2 and 10 cm on day 4.~Plot (0,4), (2,7), (4,10).~Its height rises by 3 cm every two days in these readings.',
 'Temperatures at 09:00, 10:00 and 11:00 are 12°C, 15°C and 14°C. Describe the changes and name the horizontal axis.',None,'The temperature rises by 3°C, then falls by 1°C.~The horizontal axis shows time.~The vertical axis shows temperature in °C.',
 'Time order matters; do not sort the points by height.','Time series=Values of a quantity recorded over time.~Trend=The overall pattern in data.',
 'Which belongs on the horizontal axis of a daily temperature graph?','Day.','Temperature in °C.','The hottest reading.','A time series uses time on its horizontal axis.')
 add('graph-relationships','Straight and curved relationships','A straight-line relationship has a constant gradient.~A curved relationship changes steepness.~A pattern alone does not explain its cause.',
 'Read both axis labels before describing a graph.~Compare the changes over equal horizontal intervals.~Say where a graph rises, falls or levels off.',
 'For y = 2x, x values 0, 1, 2 give y values 0, 2, 4.~For y = x² they give 0, 1, 4.~The first has equal increases; the second does not.',
 'For x = 1, 2, 3, y values are 1, 4, 9. Are the points on one straight line? Explain.',None,'The increases in y are 3 and 5.~The x increases are both 1.~The gradient changes, so the relationship is not one straight line.',
 'An increasing graph can still be curved.','Linear relationship=A relationship shown by a straight line.~Non-linear relationship=A relationship whose graph is not one straight line.',
 'Which set of y values has a constant increase when x rises by 1?','2, 5, 8, 11.','1, 4, 9, 16.','1, 2, 4, 8.','The increase is 3 each time.')
 add('fraction-order','Fractions on number lines','The denominator splits one whole into equal parts.~The numerator counts those parts.~A fraction greater than 1 lies beyond 1 on a number line.',
 'Use equal spacing for equal steps.~To compare fractions, use a common denominator or decimal.~For example, 1/2 = 3/6, which is larger than 2/6.',
 'Split 0 to 1 into four equal intervals.~The third tick after 0 is 3/4.~1 1/4 is one more quarter beyond 1.',
 'Put 2/3, 1/2 and 3/4 in order, smallest first. Show a common denominator.',None,'Use twelfths: 2/3 = 8/12, 1/2 = 6/12, 3/4 = 9/12.~Compare 6, 8 and 9.~The order is 1/2, 2/3, 3/4.',
 'More parts in a whole means each part is smaller.','Unit fraction=A fraction with numerator 1.~Common denominator=A shared denominator used to compare or combine fractions.',
 'Which is greater: 3/5 or 1/2?','3/5.','1/2.','They are equal.','3/5 = 6/10 and 1/2 = 5/10.')
 add('zero-pairs','Positive and negative counters','A positive counter stands for +1.~A negative counter stands for −1.~One of each makes zero: a zero pair.',
 'Add counters when adding a number.~Remove matching counters when subtracting.~You can add a zero pair without changing the total.',
 'Start with +2, shown by two positive counters.~To subtract −1, add a zero pair, then remove its negative counter.~Three positive counters remain: 2 − (−1) = 3.',
 'Use zero pairs to explain why 3 − (−2) = 5.',5,'Start with three positive counters.~Add two zero pairs and remove the two negative counters.~Five positive counters remain.',
 'A subtraction sign means remove; it is separate from a number’s sign.','Zero pair=A positive and negative counter together, with total zero.~Directed number=A number that can be positive, zero or negative.',
 'What is the total of four positive and three negative counters?','1.','7.','−1.','Three zero pairs cancel, leaving one positive counter.')
 add('fraction-whole','Finding the whole from a fraction','Sometimes you know a part but need the whole.~A bar can show equal parts of the whole.~Label the known parts before calculating.',
 'Divide the known amount by its number of parts.~This gives one part.~Multiply by the total number of parts.',
 'Three fifths of a number is 42.~One fifth is 42 ÷ 3 = 14.~Five fifths is 14 × 5 = 70.',
 'Four sevenths of a number is 36. Find the whole number.',63,'One seventh is 36 ÷ 4 = 9.~The whole has seven parts.~7 × 9 = 63.',
 'Do not divide by all the parts until you know the whole.','Whole=The complete amount represented by all equal parts.~Part=One amount making up a whole.',
 'Two thirds of a number is 18. What is the whole?','27.','12.','36.','One third is 9, so three thirds is 27.')
 add('compound-area','Area of compound shapes','A compound shape is made from simpler shapes.~Area counts square units inside the boundary.~A missing length can often be found by subtraction.',
 'Split the shape into rectangles or triangles.~Find each area and add them.~Or find a large area and subtract a missing piece.',
 'An L-shape fits inside an 8 cm by 6 cm rectangle.~A 3 cm by 2 cm corner is missing.~Area = 8 × 6 − 3 × 2 = 42 cm².',
 'A 10 cm by 7 cm rectangle has a 4 cm by 3 cm corner cut out. Find the remaining area.',58,'The full rectangle has area 70 cm².~The cut-out has area 12 cm².~70 − 12 = 58 cm².',
 'Do not subtract a length from an area.','Compound shape=A shape made by joining simpler shapes.~Perpendicular height=A height measured at a right angle to its base.',
 'A rectangle has area 40 cm² and a cut-out has area 6 cm². What remains?','34 cm².','46 cm².','34 cm.','Subtract the missing area: 40 − 6 = 34 cm².')
 add('timetables','Time, calendars and timetables','A timetable gives times of events such as departures.~Read the correct row, column and day.~There are 1000 milliseconds in a second.',
 'For elapsed time, count forward in useful steps.~Split a journey at midnight if needed.~Check the date when a journey ends the next day.',
 'A train leaves at 23:35 and arrives at 00:20 the next day.~There are 25 minutes to midnight and 20 more after.~The journey lasts 45 minutes.',
 'A bus leaves at 14:47 and arrives at 16:12. How many minutes is the journey?',85,'14:47 to 15:00 is 13 minutes.~15:00 to 16:00 is 60, then 12 more.~13 + 60 + 12 = 85 minutes.',
 'Clock minutes are out of 60, not 100.','Elapsed time=The time that passes between two events.~Millisecond=One thousandth of a second.',
 'How many milliseconds are in 2 seconds?','2000.','200.','0.002.','Multiply seconds by 1000.')
 add('powers-roots','Squares, cubes and roots','A square number is a whole number multiplied by itself.~A cube number multiplies three equal factors.~Triangular numbers count rows of 1, then 2, then 3, and so on.',
 'A square root undoes squaring.~A cube root undoes cubing.~Use arrays and blocks to picture the difference.',
 '5² = 25, so √25 = 5.~3³ = 27, so the cube root of 27 is 3.~The fourth triangular number is 1 + 2 + 3 + 4 = 10.',
 'Find √81 and the cube root of 64. Explain one difference between a square and a cube number.',None,'√81 = 9 because 9 × 9 = 81.~The cube root of 64 is 4 because 4 × 4 × 4 = 64.~Squares have two equal factors; cubes have three.',
 'Squaring is multiplying by itself, not multiplying by two.','Square root=A number which, multiplied by itself, gives the starting number.~Cube number=A number made by multiplying three equal integer factors.',
 'Which is the fifth triangular number?','15.','25.','10.','Add 1 + 2 + 3 + 4 + 5 = 15.')
 add('mixed','Mixed numbers and improper fractions','An improper fraction has a numerator at least as large as its denominator.~A mixed number shows whole units and a fraction.~Both can name the same point on a number line.',
 'To change a mixed number, multiply the whole number by the denominator, then add the numerator.~Keep the denominator.~To reverse this, divide the numerator by the denominator.',
 '2 3/5 = (2 × 5 + 3)/5 = 13/5.~Each whole contains five fifths.~13 ÷ 5 is 2 remainder 3, so it is 2 3/5.',
 'Write 3 2/7 as an improper fraction, then write 17/4 as a mixed number.',None,'3 × 7 + 2 = 23, so 23/7.~17 ÷ 4 is 4 remainder 1.~17/4 = 4 1/4.',
 'The denominator stays the same when changing the form.','Improper fraction=A fraction whose numerator is at least its denominator.~Mixed number=A whole number together with a proper fraction.',
 'What is 2 1/3 as an improper fraction?','7/3.','3/3.','5/3.','Two wholes contain six thirds; one more third makes seven thirds.')
 add('shapes','Naming shapes and solids','A polygon is a flat shape with straight sides.~A regular polygon has equal sides and equal angles.~A solid is three-dimensional.',
 'Name triangles using their sides or angles.~Count faces, edges and vertices on solids.~A prism has an unchanged cross-section along its length.',
 'An equilateral triangle has three equal sides.~A cuboid has six rectangular faces, twelve edges and eight vertices.~A cylinder has a circular cross-section along its length.',
 'Name a triangle with two equal sides. State the numbers of faces, edges and vertices on a cube.',None,'The triangle is isosceles.~A cube has 6 faces and 12 edges.~It has 8 vertices.',
 'A regular polygon needs equal angles as well as equal sides.','Vertex=A corner where edges meet; the plural is vertices.~Prism=A solid with a constant cross-section along its length.',
 'Which triangle has three equal sides?','Equilateral.','Scalene.','Right-angled.','Equilateral means all three sides have equal length.')
 add('ratio-missing','Ratio problems with a part or difference','A ratio compares equal-sized parts.~Sometimes a single amount is known.~Sometimes the difference between amounts is known.',
 'Draw the parts as equal boxes.~Match the known amount to the right number of boxes.~Find one box, then find the amounts needed.',
 'Red:blue = 2:5 and there are 18 more blue.~The difference is 5 − 2 = 3 parts, so each part is 6.~There are 12 red and 30 blue.',
 'A:B = 3:7. B is 24 greater than A. Find A and B.',None,'The difference is 4 parts, so one part is 24 ÷ 4 = 6.~A = 18.~B = 42.',
 'When given the difference, use the difference in parts, not the total parts.','Unitary method=Finding one part or one item first.~Ratio part=One of the equal-sized shares represented by the ratio numbers.',
 'A:B = 2:5 and A = 14. What is B?','35.','21.','70.','Two parts are 14, so one part is 7 and five parts are 35.')
 add('direct','Direct proportion','In direct proportion, both quantities scale by the same multiplier.~Doubling one doubles the other.~The graph is a straight line through the origin.',
 'Use a ratio table or double number line.~Find the amount for one item first if useful.~A fixed starting charge means the total cost is not directly proportional.',
 'Three identical pens cost £4.50.~One pen costs £1.50.~Eight pens cost £12, assuming the same price for every pen.',
 'Five identical books cost £12.50. Find the cost of eight books. State the assumption.',20,'One book costs £2.50.~Eight books cost 8 × £2.50 = £20.~The price per book stays the same, with no extra fee.',
 'A straight line that misses the origin does not show direct proportion.','Direct proportion=A relationship in which quantities have a constant ratio.~Origin=The point (0, 0) where the axes cross.',
 'Which rule shows direct proportion?','y = 4x.','y = x + 4.','y = 4/x.','For y = 4x, the ratio y/x stays 4 and the graph passes through the origin.')
 add('conversion-graphs','Conversion graphs and currencies','A conversion graph links two units or currencies.~Use the axis labels to choose a direction.~A stated constant rate gives a straight line through zero.',
 'Use a known pair to find the multiplier.~Multiply in one direction and divide in the other.~Use only the rate stated in the question.',
 'At a stated rate of £1 = €1.25, £20 = €25.~To change €45 to pounds, divide by 1.25.~€45 = £36 at this example rate.',
 'At a stated rate of £1 = €1.20, change €54 into pounds. Ignore fees.',45,'Each £1 is worth €1.20.~Calculate 54 ÷ 1.20.~The amount is £45.',
 'Do not multiply in both directions.','Conversion graph=A graph linking values measured in two different units.~Exchange rate=A stated relationship between two currencies.',
 'At £1 = €1.50, how many euros equal £8?','€12.','€5.33.','€9.50.','Multiply 8 by 1.50 to get 12.')
 add('similarity','Similar shapes and matching lengths','Similar shapes have the same shape but may differ in size.~Matching angles are equal.~Matching lengths have one common scale factor.',
 'Identify matching sides before dividing.~Find the scale factor from a known pair.~Apply it to another matching length.',
 'A small rectangle is 3 cm by 5 cm.~A similar rectangle has its 3 cm side enlarged to 12 cm: factor 4.~Its other side is 5 × 4 = 20 cm.',
 'Similar triangles have matching sides 6 cm and 15 cm. Another small side is 8 cm. Find its matching large side.',20,'The scale factor is 15 ÷ 6 = 2.5.~Multiply the matching length by 2.5.~8 × 2.5 = 20 cm.',
 'Match corresponding sides, not just the nearest labels.','Similar shapes=Shapes with equal matching angles and proportional matching lengths.~Scale factor=The multiplier taking one set of lengths to another.',
 'Lengths scale by 3. What happens to area?','It scales by 9.','It scales by 3.','It scales by 6.','Both dimensions scale by 3, so area scales by 3² = 9.')
 add('factorise','Factorising a single bracket','Factorising rewrites a sum as a product.~It is the reverse of expanding.~A common factor divides every term.',
 'Find a shared number or letter factor.~Write it outside a bracket.~Divide each original term by that factor to fill the bracket.',
 'For 6x + 15, the largest shared number factor is 3.~6x ÷ 3 = 2x and 15 ÷ 3 = 5.~So 6x + 15 = 3(2x + 5).',
 'Factorise 12x + 18 fully.',None,'The largest shared factor is 6.~12x ÷ 6 = 2x and 18 ÷ 6 = 3.~The answer is 6(2x + 3).',
 'Check by expanding; every original term must return.','Factorise=Rewrite an expression as a product of factors.~Common factor=A factor shared by every term being factorised.',
 'Which is 8x + 12 fully factorised?','4(2x + 3).','4(2x + 12).','2(4x + 6).','4 is the greatest common factor; the other factor is 2x + 3.')
 add('fraction-model','Picturing multiplication and division of fractions','Multiplying by a fraction finds part of an amount.~An area grid can show a fraction of a fraction.~Division can ask how many equal pieces fit.',
 'For multiplication, split both directions into equal parts.~Count the overlapping parts.~For division, count groups of the divisor’s size.',
 'Half of three quarters is 3/8: shade three columns of four, then half their height.~Three quarters divided by one quarter asks how many quarter-pieces fit.~There are three, so 3/4 ÷ 1/4 = 3.',
 'Use equal pieces to explain 2/3 ÷ 1/6. Then calculate 2/3 × 1/2.',None,'2/3 = 4/6, so four sixth-sized pieces fit.~The division answer is 4.~2/3 × 1/2 = 2/6 = 1/3.',
 'Dividing by a small positive fraction can make the answer larger.','Reciprocal=The multiplicative inverse of a non-zero number.~Area model=A rectangle split into equal parts to show a calculation.',
 'How many quarters fit into 1 1/2?','6.','3.','3/8.','1 1/2 = 6/4, which contains six quarters.')
 add('reflection','Reflecting a whole shape','Reflection flips a shape across a mirror line.~Each point and its image are equally far from the line.~The joining segment meets the mirror line at a right angle.',
 'Reflect each vertex separately.~Count perpendicular distances to the mirror line.~Join the image vertices in the same order.',
 'Reflect (2, 1) in the vertical line x = 4.~The point is two units left of the line, so its image is two units right.~The image is (6, 1).',
 'Reflect the triangle (1,1), (3,1), (1,2) in the y-axis. Give all three image vertices.',None,'The y-axis is x = 0.~Keep y and reverse the sign of x.~The images are (−1,1), (−3,1), (−1,2).',
 'Reflecting in y = x swaps coordinates; it does not just change a sign.','Mirror line=The line across which a shape is reflected.~Image=The result of transforming a shape.',
 'Reflect (2,5) in y = x. What is its image?','(5,2).','(−2,5).','(2,−5).','Reflection in y = x swaps x and y.')
 add('fractional-equations','Equations with brackets and fractions','An equation says two expressions have equal value.~Brackets and fraction bars group terms.~The same operation on both sides keeps the equality.',
 'Clear a denominator by multiplying both sides.~Expand brackets carefully.~Undo the remaining operations and check the answer.',
 '(x + 4)/3 = 5.~Multiply both sides by 3: x + 4 = 15.~Subtract 4: x = 11.',
 'Solve (2x − 1)/3 = 7 and check your answer.',11,'Multiply by 3: 2x − 1 = 21.~Add 1, then divide by 2: x = 11.~Check: (22 − 1)/3 = 7.',
 'A fraction bar divides the whole expression above it.','Solution=A value that makes an equation true.~Inverse operation=An operation that undoes another operation.',
 'Solve (x + 2)/4 = 3.','x = 10.','x = 1.','x = 14.','Multiply by 4, then subtract 2: x = 12 − 2 = 10.')
 add('indices-laws','Index laws with the same base','An index tells you how many equal factors to multiply.~For example, x³ = x × x × x.~The base is the repeated number or letter.',
 'When multiplying the same base, add the powers.~When dividing the same non-zero base, subtract the powers.~For a power of a power, multiply the powers.',
 'x³ × x² has five x factors, so it is x⁵.~x⁷ ÷ x² = x⁵, for x ≠ 0.~(x²)³ = x⁶.',
 'Simplify a⁴ × a³ ÷ a². State a restriction.',None,'Add the powers when multiplying: a⁷.~Subtract 2 when dividing: a⁵.~a cannot be zero because a² is a divisor.',
 'Adding x² + x³ does not give x⁵; these are unlike terms.','Base=The repeated factor in a power.~Index=The power indicating repeated multiplication.',
 'Simplify x² × x⁴.','x⁶.','x⁸.','2x⁶.','There are 2 + 4 = 6 factors of x.')
 add('powers-ten','Positive and negative powers of ten','10³ = 1000 and 10¹ = 10.~10⁰ = 1.~A negative power gives a reciprocal: 10⁻² = 1/100.',
 'Multiplying by 10 makes every digit worth ten times as much.~Dividing by 10 makes every digit worth one tenth as much.~The decimal point marks the units position; it is the digits’ place values that change.',
 '4.2 × 10³ = 4200.~4.2 × 10⁻³ = 0.0042.~In each step the digits move to a different place value.',
 'Write 10⁻⁴ as a decimal, then calculate 6.3 × 10⁻².',0.063,'10⁻⁴ = 1/10 000 = 0.0001.~10⁻² means divide by 100.~6.3 ÷ 100 = 0.063.',
 'A negative power does not mean a negative answer.','Negative index=A power representing a reciprocal.~Power of ten=A number such as 1000 = 10³ or 0.01 = 10⁻².',
 'What is 10⁻³?','0.001.','−1000.','0.003.','10⁻³ = 1/1000 = 0.001.')
 add('standard-read','Reading and writing standard form','Standard form writes a number as A × 10ⁿ.~For a positive number, 1 ≤ A < 10.~n is an integer, which may be negative or zero.',
 'Choose A with one non-zero digit before the decimal point.~Find the power of ten that restores the original number.~Check whether the result should be large or small.',
 '56 000 = 5.6 × 10⁴.~0.00056 = 5.6 × 10⁻⁴.~The coefficient is the same, but the power changes the size.',
 'Write 0.00072 in standard form and write 3.4 × 10⁵ as an ordinary number.',None,'0.00072 = 7.2 × 10⁻⁴.~The coefficient 7.2 is between 1 and 10.~3.4 × 10⁵ = 340 000.',
 'The coefficient must be less than 10, not equal to 10.','Standard form=A × 10ⁿ, where 1 ≤ A < 10 for a positive number and n is an integer.~Coefficient=The numerical multiplier of a term.',
 'Which is 0.0048 in standard form?','4.8 × 10⁻³.','48 × 10⁻⁴.','4.8 × 10³.','4.8 divided by 1000 is 0.0048, and 4.8 is an allowed coefficient.')
 add('data-types','Types of data and sensible samples','Categorical data are labels such as eye colour.~Discrete numerical data are counted in separate values.~Continuous data are measured and can take values between readings.',
 'Choose data that answer the question.~Use a sample that fairly represents the group.~Check unusual results for recording errors before making a decision.',
 'Number of siblings is discrete: 0, 1, 2 and so on.~Height is continuous, even if rounded to a centimetre.~Favourite sport is categorical.',
 'Classify shoe colour, number of goals and journey time as categorical, discrete or continuous.',None,'Shoe colour is categorical.~Number of goals is discrete.~Journey time is continuous.',
 'Rounded measurements do not become discrete just because whole numbers are recorded.','Discrete data=Numerical data with separate possible values, often counts.~Continuous data=Measurements that can take any value in a range.',
 'Which is continuous data?','Mass of an apple.','Number of apples.','Colour of an apple.','Mass is measured and can take values between whole numbers.')
 add('grouped','Grouped data and estimates','Grouping puts values into intervals.~Discrete values are counted; continuous values are measured.~Grouping hides the exact values inside each interval.',
 'Use intervals that do not overlap.~For an estimated mean, use each class midpoint.~Multiply midpoint by frequency, add, then divide by total frequency.',
 'Heights 140 ≤ h < 150 have frequency 2; 150 ≤ h < 160 have frequency 3.~Midpoints are 145 and 155.~Estimated mean = (145 × 2 + 155 × 3)/5 = 151 cm.',
 'The intervals 0 ≤ t < 10 and 10 ≤ t < 20 have frequencies 3 and 7. Estimate the mean time.',12,'Use midpoints 5 and 15.~Weighted total = 5 × 3 + 15 × 7 = 120.~Estimated mean = 120 ÷ 10 = 12.',
 'The result is an estimate because the individual values are unknown.','Class interval=A range used to group data.~Class midpoint=The value halfway between a class’s boundaries.',
 'What is the midpoint of 20 ≤ x < 30?','25.','10.','50.','Average the boundaries: (20 + 30)/2 = 25.')
 add('compare-data','Comparing data and choosing an average','An average describes a typical value.~The range describes spread.~A fair comparison considers both.',
 'Choose a mean or median that suits the data.~An extreme value can affect the mean strongly.~Use the mode for the most common category.',
 'Group A has median 12 and range 3; B has median 10 and range 8.~A has the higher typical value.~A is also less spread out, using these measures.',
 'Daily journeys have median 25 minutes and range 8 for route A; median 20 and range 18 for route B. Compare them.',None,'Route B has a shorter typical journey using the median.~Route A has less variation using the range.~The full data would give more detail than these two measures.',
 'A smaller range does not mean a smaller average.','Distribution=The way data values are spread.~Outlier=A value far away from the main pattern of the data.',
 'Which average is usually less affected by one very high salary?','Median.','Mean.','Total.','The median uses position in the ordered list, rather than sharing the total.')
 add('quadrilaterals','Quadrilaterals and geometric reasons','A parallelogram has two pairs of parallel opposite sides.~A rectangle has four right angles.~A rhombus has four equal sides; a square is both a rhombus and a rectangle.',
 'Use marked facts rather than the appearance of a sketch.~Opposite angles in a parallelogram are equal.~Diagonals of a parallelogram bisect each other.',
 'One angle in a parallelogram is 68°.~The next angle is 180 − 68 = 112°.~The opposite angles repeat 68° and 112°.',
 'A parallelogram has an angle of 73°. Find the other three angles and explain the rule.',None,'The opposite angle is 73°.~Adjacent angles sum to 180°, so the others are 107°.~The three angles are 73°, 107° and 107°.',
 'A diagram can be out of scale; use the stated properties.','Bisect=Divide into two equal parts.~Parallelogram=A quadrilateral with both pairs of opposite sides parallel.',
 'What does it mean when diagonals bisect each other?','Each cuts the other into equal halves.','They must cross at 90°.','All four sections must be equal.','Each diagonal is halved; the two diagonals need not have the same length.')
 add('sample-space','Sample spaces','A sample space lists every possible outcome.~A table can keep two choices organised.~For equally likely outcomes, probability = favourable outcomes ÷ all outcomes.',
 'List every first choice down one side.~List every second choice across the top.~Fill every cell, without missing or repeating an outcome.',
 'Toss a fair coin and roll a fair die.~There are 2 × 6 = 12 equally likely pairs.~Head and an even number has 3 pairs, so its probability is 3/12 = 1/4.',
 'Roll two fair dice. How many ordered outcomes are possible? How many have a total of 7?',None,'There are 6 × 6 = 36 ordered outcomes.~Six pairs total 7: (1,6), (2,5), (3,4), (4,3), (5,2), (6,1).~The probability is 6/36 = 1/6.',
 'Totals from two dice are not all equally likely.','Sample space=The set of every possible outcome.~Equally likely=Having the same chance of happening.',
 'A coin has 2 outcomes and a spinner has 4. How many pairs are possible?','8.','6.','4.','Every coin outcome pairs with each spinner outcome: 2 × 4 = 8.')
 add('experiment','Probability experiments','An experiment records what actually happens.~Relative frequency = event count ÷ number of trials.~It estimates a probability from results.',
 'Keep a tally and count all trials.~More trials usually make the estimate more stable.~Even a fair random experiment can give uneven results.',
 'A spinner lands on red 18 times in 50 spins.~Relative frequency of red = 18/50 = 0.36.~This does not guarantee 36 reds in the next 100 spins.',
 'A drawing pin lands point-up 42 times in 120 trials. Estimate the probability of point-up.',0.35,'Use event count divided by total trials.~42 ÷ 120 = 0.35.~This is an estimate based on the experiment.',
 'A short run of results does not prove an object is fair or unfair.','Relative frequency=The fraction of trials in which an event occurs.~Trial=One run of a probability experiment.',
 'Red occurs 12 times in 40 trials. What is its relative frequency?','0.3.','0.12.','3.33.','12 ÷ 40 = 0.3.')
 add('frequency-tree','Frequency trees','A frequency tree splits a total into groups.~The numbers are counts, not probabilities.~Each pair of branches must add to its parent total.',
 'Write the starting total.~Split into categories, then split each category again.~For a probability, use the total for the group being asked about.',
 'Of 30 pupils, 18 walk and 12 do not.~Of the walkers, 10 carry a bag and 8 do not.~The probability a walker carries a bag is 10/18 = 5/9.',
 'Of 40 pupils, 25 take a bus. Of the bus users, 9 have a pass. How many bus users have no pass? What fraction of bus users have a pass?',None,'25 − 9 = 16 bus users have no pass.~There are 25 bus users in the selected group.~The fraction is 9/25.',
 'Selecting bus users changes the denominator to the bus-user total.','Frequency tree=A branching diagram showing counts in groups.~Parent total=The count before a group is split into smaller groups.',
 'A group of 24 splits into 9 and one other branch. What is missing?','15.','33.','9.','Both branches must add to 24, so 24 − 9 = 15.')
 add('circle-parts','Parts of circles and curved boundaries','A sector is a slice of a circle.~Its angle gives its fraction of the full 360° turn.~A curved edge is called an arc.',
 'Multiply the full-circle area by angle/360.~For a sector’s perimeter, add the arc and its two radii.~For a semicircle, add the curved half-circumference and the diameter.',
 'A semicircle has radius 4 cm.~Its curved edge is half of 2π × 4 = 8π, so 4π cm.~Its full perimeter is 4π + 8 cm.',
 'A quarter-circle has radius 6 cm. Give its exact area and full perimeter.',None,'Area = (1/4) × π × 6² = 9π cm².~Arc length = (1/4) × 2π × 6 = 3π cm.~Perimeter = 3π + 12 cm.',
 'A sector’s perimeter includes its straight edges.','Sector=A region between two radii and an arc.~Arc=A part of a circle’s circumference.',
 'What fraction of a full circle is a 60° sector?','1/6.','1/3.','1/60.','60/360 simplifies to 1/6.')
 add('number-sets','Integers, rational and irrational numbers','Integers are whole numbers, including negatives and zero.~Rational numbers can be written as a fraction of integers with a non-zero denominator.~Irrational numbers cannot be written that way.',
 'Terminating decimals are rational.~Recurring decimals are also rational.~Roots of non-square positive integers are irrational.',
 '−4 is an integer and rational: −4 = −4/1.~0.125 is rational: it is 1/8.~√2 is irrational, but √9 = 3 is rational.',
 'Classify −6, 0.4 and √5. Which are integers and which are rational?',None,'−6 is an integer and rational.~0.4 = 2/5 is rational, but not an integer.~√5 is irrational and not an integer.',
 'A root sign alone does not make a number irrational.','Rational number=A number expressible as a fraction of integers, with a non-zero denominator.~Irrational number=A real number that cannot be written as such a fraction.',
 'Which number is irrational?','√7.','√16.','0.333….','7 is not a square number, so √7 cannot be written as a fraction of integers.')
 add('repeated-change','Repeated percentage changes','Each percentage change acts on the current amount.~An increase of 5% uses multiplier 1.05.~Repeating the change means multiplying again.',
 'Write a multiplier for each step.~Multiply the starting amount by every multiplier.~Round money at the end unless told otherwise.',
 '£200 grows by 10%, then another 10%.~£200 × 1.1 × 1.1 = £242.~The total increase is 21%, because the second rise acts on £220.',
 'A £500 item loses 20% of its value each year for two years. Find its value.',320,'A 20% fall uses multiplier 0.8.~500 × 0.8² = 320.~The value is £320.',
 'Two 10% increases are not one 20% increase.','Multiplier=A number you multiply by to apply a scale or percentage change.~Depreciation=A fall in value over time.',
 'What multiplier represents two successive 20% decreases?','0.64.','0.60.','1.44.','Multiply 0.8 by 0.8 to get 0.64.')
 add('interest','Simple and compound interest','Interest is an amount added to savings or charged on borrowing.~Simple interest uses the starting amount every time.~Compound interest uses the changing balance.',
 'For simple interest, find one period’s interest and multiply by the number of periods.~For compound interest, repeat the percentage multiplier.~Use the fixed example rate stated in the question.',
 '£300 earns 4% simple interest per year: £12 each year.~After three years the balance is £336.~With annual compounding it is £300 × 1.04³ = £337.46 to the nearest penny.',
 '£400 earns 5% compound interest each year for two years. Find the final balance.',441,'Use multiplier 1.05.~400 × 1.05² = 441.~The final balance is £441.',
 'Compound interest can earn interest on earlier interest.','Principal=The starting amount saved or borrowed.~Compound interest=Interest calculated on the changing balance.',
 '£100 earns 10% simple interest per year. What is the balance after 2 years?','£120.','£121.','£110.','Simple interest adds £10 each year: £100 + £20.')
 add('nets-surface','Nets and surface area','A net unfolds a solid’s outside into flat faces.~Surface area is the total area of these faces.~Volume measures the space inside instead.',
 'Sketch or list every face.~Find each face’s area.~Add them, counting equal faces the correct number of times.',
 'A cuboid is 4 cm by 3 cm by 2 cm.~Its three face pairs have areas 12, 8 and 6 cm².~Surface area = 2(12 + 8 + 6) = 52 cm².',
 'A closed cuboid is 5 cm by 4 cm by 3 cm. Find its surface area.',94,'The face areas are 20, 15 and 12 cm².~Each appears twice.~2(20 + 15 + 12) = 94 cm².',
 'Use square units for surface area, not cubic units.','Net=A flat arrangement of faces that folds to make a solid.~Surface area=The total area of a solid’s outside faces.',
 'A cube has edge 3 cm. What is its surface area?','54 cm².','27 cm².','18 cm².','There are six faces, each of area 3 × 3 = 9 cm².')
 add('solid-volume','Volumes of cones, pyramids and spheres','A cone or pyramid has one third of the volume of a prism with the same base area and height.~A sphere has volume 4πr³/3.~These are extra challenge formulae.',
 'Use the perpendicular height of a cone or pyramid.~Square the radius for the base area of a cone.~Keep π until the final calculation.',
 'A cone has radius 3 cm and perpendicular height 8 cm.~Volume = (1/3) × π × 3² × 8.~The exact volume is 24π cm³.',
 'A pyramid has base area 30 cm² and perpendicular height 9 cm. Find its volume.',90,'Use one third × base area × perpendicular height.~(1/3) × 30 × 9 = 90.~The volume is 90 cm³.',
 'Sloping height is not perpendicular height.','Perpendicular height=The shortest height measured at a right angle to the base.~Sphere=A solid whose surface points are all the same distance from its centre.',
 'A cone and cylinder have the same base and height. How do their volumes compare?','The cone has one third of the cylinder’s volume.','They are equal.','The cone has half the cylinder’s volume.','The cone formula includes a factor of one third.')
 add('flow-rates','Flow rates and filling graphs','A flow rate describes how much liquid moves in a unit of time.~For a constant rate, volume = rate × time.~A volume–time graph can show filling or draining.',
 'Check the time and volume units.~The graph’s gradient gives net flow rate.~If water enters and leaves, subtract the outflow from the inflow.',
 'Water enters at 8 litres per minute and leaves at 3 litres per minute.~Net filling rate is 5 litres per minute.~After 6 minutes, the volume has increased by 30 litres.',
 'A tank starts with 12 litres. Water flows in at 7 litres/min and out at 2 litres/min. How much is in the tank after 4 minutes?',32,'Net rate = 7 − 2 = 5 litres/min.~Added volume = 5 × 4 = 20 litres.~12 + 20 = 32 litres.',
 'A starting volume changes the intercept, not the constant filling gradient.','Flow rate=The volume moving per unit time.~Net rate=The overall rate after combining increases and decreases.',
 'A tank gains 24 litres in 6 minutes at a constant rate. What is the rate?','4 litres/min.','144 litres/min.','0.25 litres/min.','Divide the volume change by the time: 24 ÷ 6 = 4.')
 add('compound-units','Converting compound units','Compound units combine two kinds of measurement.~Speed might be in metres per second or kilometres per hour.~Convert both parts carefully.',
 'Write the distance and time conversions.~Apply them one at a time.~For speed, 1 m/s = 3.6 km/h.',
 '10 m/s is 10 × 3600 = 36 000 metres per hour.~36 000 m = 36 km.~So 10 m/s = 36 km/h.',
 'Convert 72 km/h into metres per second.',20,'72 km = 72 000 m.~One hour is 3600 seconds.~72 000 ÷ 3600 = 20 m/s.',
 'Changing kilometres to metres alone is not enough.','Compound unit=A unit combining different measurements, such as m/s.~Rate of change=The change in one quantity per unit change in another.',
 'Convert 5 m/s into km/h.','18 km/h.','1.39 km/h.','5000 km/h.','Multiply by 3.6: 5 × 3.6 = 18.')
 add('standard-calc','Calculating in standard form','Standard form separates a coefficient and a power of ten.~Use index laws when multiplying or dividing.~For addition and subtraction, first use matching powers.',
 'Multiply or divide the coefficients and powers separately.~For addition, rewrite both with the same power of ten.~Finish with a coefficient between 1 and 10.',
 '(3 × 10⁵) + (4 × 10⁴) = (3 × 10⁵) + (0.4 × 10⁵).~Add the coefficients: 3.4 × 10⁵.~The powers are not added in an addition calculation.',
 'Calculate (6 × 10⁷) ÷ (3 × 10²), then (2 × 10⁴) + (5 × 10³). Give standard form.',None,'For division, 6 ÷ 3 = 2 and 10⁷ ÷ 10² = 10⁵, so 2 × 10⁵.~For addition, 5 × 10³ = 0.5 × 10⁴.~The sum is 2.5 × 10⁴.',
 'Adding powers is a multiplication rule, not an addition rule.','Scientific notation=Another name for standard form.~Normalise=Rewrite a standard-form answer with its coefficient in the allowed range.',
 'What is (2 × 10³) + (3 × 10²)?','2.3 × 10³.','5 × 10⁵.','5 × 10³.','2000 + 300 = 2300 = 2.3 × 10³.')
 add('money-bank','Bank balances and ways to pay','Money paid in is a credit; money taken out is a debit.~A negative balance means money is owed.~A debit card spends account money; a credit card involves borrowing.',
 'Start with the opening balance.~Add credits and subtract debits in order.~Check whether the example includes fees or interest.',
 'An account starts at £45.~A payment of £60 leaves −£15.~A deposit of £25 then gives a balance of £10.',
 'An account starts at −£18. £70 is paid in and £39 is spent. Find the final balance.',13,'−18 + 70 = 52.~52 − 39 = 13.~The final balance is £13.',
 'A credit-card spending limit is not money saved.','Credit=Money paid into an account.~Debit=Money taken out of an account.',
 'A balance is −£12 and £30 is paid in. What is the new balance?','£18.','£42.','−£42.','−12 + 30 = 18.')
 add('money-pay','Pay, deductions and VAT','Gross pay is pay before deductions.~Net pay is what remains afterwards.~VAT is a tax added at a stated rate in a question.',
 'For hourly pay, multiply hours by the hourly rate.~Subtract each stated deduction.~To include VAT, use the appropriate percentage multiplier.',
 'Six hours at £12 per hour gives £72 gross pay.~If stated deductions are £8, net pay is £64.~An item costing £40 before 20% VAT costs £48 including VAT.',
 'A worker earns £13 per hour for 8 hours. Stated deductions are £17. Find the net pay.',87,'Gross pay = 13 × 8 = £104.~Subtract £17.~Net pay is £87.',
 'Use the rates supplied in the example; real pay deductions depend on the situation.','Gross pay=Pay before deductions.~Net pay=Pay remaining after deductions.',
 'An item is £50 before a stated 20% VAT. What is the price including VAT?','£60.','£40.','£70.','20% of £50 is £10, so the total is £60.')
 add('money-budget','Budgets and running costs','A budget plans income and spending.~A fixed cost stays the same over the period.~A variable cost changes with use or sales.',
 'Put all figures on the same time basis.~Add income and expenses separately.~Subtract spending from income to find a surplus or shortfall.',
 'A club receives £160 in a week.~It spends £90 on hire and £45 on materials.~The surplus is £160 − £135 = £25.',
 'Monthly income is £850. Costs are £420 rent, £130 food, £75 travel and £160 other costs. Find the monthly surplus.',65,'Total costs = 420 + 130 + 75 + 160 = £785.~850 − 785 = £65.~There is a £65 surplus.',
 'Weekly and monthly costs cannot be added without converting them.','Budget=A plan comparing income and spending over a period.~Surplus=The amount by which income exceeds spending.',
 'Income is £300 and spending is £325. What is the result?','A £25 shortfall.','A £25 surplus.','A £625 surplus.','Spending exceeds income by £25.')
 add('money-save','Saving, investing and comparing value','Saving sets money aside for future use.~Investments can rise or fall in value.~Compare costs using the same quantity or time period.',
 'Use a unit price to compare different pack sizes.~For savings examples, check the time and interest method.~A larger possible return can come with greater uncertainty.',
 'A 750 g pack costs £3; a 1 kg pack costs £4.20.~The first costs £4 per kg.~It has the lower unit price, assuming both products suit the need.',
 'A 600 g pack costs £2.40 and a 1 kg pack costs £4.50. Compare their prices per kilogram.',None,'The first costs 2.40 ÷ 0.6 = £4 per kg.~The second costs £4.50 per kg.~The 600 g pack has the lower unit price.',
 'The lowest pack price does not always give the lowest unit price.','Unit price=The cost for one unit, such as one kilogram.~Investment=Money put into something whose value may rise or fall.',
 '£240 is saved in 12 equal deposits. What is each deposit?','£20.','£2880.','£12.','Divide the target by the number of equal deposits: 240 ÷ 12 = 20.')
 add('money-borrow','Borrowing and total repayment','Borrowed money must be repaid.~Interest and fees can make the total larger than the loan.~A mortgage is borrowing secured on a property.',
 'Multiply the number of payments by each payment.~Add any separate fee.~Subtract the amount borrowed to find the extra cost.',
 'An example loan of £600 has twelve payments of £55 and a £15 separate fee.~Total repayment = 12 × £55 + £15 = £675.~The extra cost is £75.',
 'An example loan of £800 has 10 payments of £86 and a separate £20 fee. Find the total repaid and the extra cost.',None,'10 × £86 + £20 = £880 repaid.~£880 − £800 = £80 extra.~This compares the full stated cost, not just each payment.',
 'A smaller monthly payment can still give a larger total cost.','Loan=Money borrowed under an agreement to repay.~Mortgage=A loan secured on a property.',
 'A £500 loan is repaid through 12 payments of £45, with no other fee. What is the extra cost?','£40.','£540.','£5.','Total repayment is £540, which is £40 more than £500.')
 add('money-overseas','Spending overseas','Currency questions use a stated exchange rate.~Fees may change the amount available.~Rates in these lessons are fixed examples.',
 'Work out whether to multiply or divide.~Apply fees in the order stated.~Keep enough decimal places before rounding money.',
 'At the example rate £1 = €1.20, £150 converts to €180.~A €6 fee is deducted afterwards.~The traveller receives €174.',
 'At £1 = €1.25, £200 is exchanged. A €5 fee is then deducted. How many euros are received?',245,'£200 converts to 200 × 1.25 = €250.~Subtract the €5 fee.~€245 is received.',
 'Check which currency the fee uses.','Exchange fee=A charge for changing currency.~Currency=The money used in a country or monetary area.',
 'At £1 = €1.10, what is £40 before fees?','€44.','€36.36.','€41.10.','40 × 1.10 = 44.')
 add('money-insurance','Insurance costs and cover','Insurance charges a premium for stated cover.~An excess is the part of a covered claim paid by the customer.~Policies can differ in what they cover.',
 'Compare total premiums over the same period.~Read the example’s cover and excess.~Use the stated rule to split a claim.',
 'An example policy costs £9 each month.~Its yearly premium is 12 × £9 = £108.~For a fully covered £500 loss with £100 excess, the insurer pays £400.',
 'An example policy costs £11 monthly. A fully covered £350 claim has an £80 excess. Find the annual premium and insurer’s payment.',None,'Annual premium = 12 × £11 = £132.~Insurer’s payment = £350 − £80 = £270.~This uses the cover stated in the example.',
 'The premium and the excess are different costs.','Premium=The price paid for an insurance policy.~Excess=The stated part of a claim paid by the policyholder.',
 'A fully covered £600 claim has a £150 excess. How much does the insurer pay?','£450.','£750.','£150.','Subtract the customer’s £150 share from £600.')
 add('line-equation','Finding a straight-line equation','A straight line can be written y = mx + c unless it is vertical.~m is the gradient.~c is the y-intercept.',
 'Read two clear points from the graph.~Calculate rise divided by horizontal run.~Use the intercept, or substitute a point, to find c.',
 'A line passes through (0, −2) and (3, 4).~Its gradient is (4 − (−2))/(3 − 0) = 2.~Its equation is y = 2x − 2.',
 'A line passes through (0,3) and (4,11). Find its equation.',None,'Gradient = (11 − 3)/(4 − 0) = 2.~The y-intercept is 3.~The equation is y = 2x + 3.',
 'The y-intercept is where x is zero, not where y is zero.','Gradient=The change in y divided by the change in x.~Y-intercept=Where a graph crosses the y-axis.',
 'What is the y-intercept of y = −3x + 5?','5.','−3.','−5/3.','Set x = 0, giving y = 5.')
 add('graph-inequalities','Shading a linear inequality','A graph inequality describes a region of points.~Its boundary is the matching equation.~A solid boundary includes equality; a dashed boundary excludes it.',
 'Draw the boundary first.~Test a point away from the boundary.~Shade the side whose points satisfy the inequality.',
 'For y > x + 1, draw y = x + 1 dashed.~(0,0) gives 0 > 1, which is false.~Shade above the line, away from (0,0).',
 'Describe how to draw the region y ≤ 2x + 3. Is (0,0) included?',None,'Draw y = 2x + 3 with a solid line.~Shade below the line, including the boundary.~(0,0) is included because 0 ≤ 3.',
 'A dashed boundary excludes every point on that line.','Boundary line=The line separating the two sides of a linear inequality.~Region=A set of points satisfying a condition.',
 'Does the boundary of y < x use a solid or dashed line?','Dashed.','Solid.','Either, with no difference.','The strict inequality excludes equality, so the boundary is dashed.')
 add('construct-triangle','Constructing triangles','A construction uses given facts to draw a shape accurately.~SSS gives three sides; SAS gives two sides and their included angle.~ASA gives two angles and their included side.',
 'Draw a given side first.~Use a protractor for a given angle and compass arcs for a given distance.~Join the intersection to finish the triangle.',
 'To construct sides 6 cm, 5 cm and 4 cm, draw a 6 cm base.~Draw an arc of radius 5 cm from one end and 4 cm from the other.~Join an arc intersection to both base ends.',
 'Describe how to construct a triangle with sides 7 cm, 5 cm and 4 cm using a ruler and compasses.',None,'Draw the 7 cm side.~Draw radius-5 cm and radius-4 cm arcs from its ends.~Join their intersection to both ends.',
 'Keep the compass width fixed while drawing each arc.','SSS=Three specified side lengths used to define a triangle.~Included angle=The angle between two specified sides.',
 'Which triangle information is SAS?','Two sides and the angle between them.','Three angles only.','Two angles and the side between them.','SAS means side, included angle, side.')
 add('bisectors','Compass constructions and bisectors','A perpendicular bisector cuts a segment in half at 90°.~An angle bisector splits an angle into equal angles.~Equal-radius arcs locate points equally far from their centres.',
 'For a perpendicular bisector, draw matching arcs from both endpoints.~Choose a radius greater than half the segment.~Join the two arc intersections with a straight line.',
 'For a segment AB of length 8 cm, choose radius 5 cm.~Draw arcs above and below from A and B without changing the width.~The line through the crossings passes through the midpoint at 90°.',
 'Explain how to construct an angle bisector using a ruler and compasses.',None,'Draw one arc from the vertex crossing both angle arms.~From those two crossings, draw equal-radius arcs that meet inside the angle.~Join their intersection to the original vertex.',
 'Measuring a midpoint alone does not show the compass construction.','Perpendicular bisector=A line cutting a segment into equal halves at a right angle.~Angle bisector=A line or ray splitting an angle into equal angles.',
 'Where is a point on the perpendicular bisector of AB?','Equally far from A and B.','Always halfway between A and B.','Always on segment AB.','Every point on this line has the same distance to the two endpoints.')
 add('congruence','Congruent shapes and triangles','Congruent shapes have the same size and shape.~Turning or reflecting a shape does not change congruence.~Corresponding sides and angles match.',
 'Triangle tests include SSS, SAS, ASA and RHS.~RHS needs a right angle, matching hypotenuse and one other matching side.~AAA proves similarity, but does not fix size.',
 'Two triangles each have sides 4 cm, 5 cm and 6 cm.~They are congruent by SSS.~Two triangles with angles 40°, 60°, 80° may have different side lengths.',
 'Two triangles have matching sides 5 cm and 8 cm and an included angle of 50°. Are they congruent? Explain.',None,'The given angle lies between the two given sides.~The triangles are congruent by SAS.~The three facts fix size and shape.',
 'Three matching angles alone do not prove congruence.','Congruent=Exactly the same size and shape.~Corresponding=Matching in position between two shapes.',
 'Which test proves congruence?','SSS.','AAA.','Two sides only.','Three matching side lengths fix a triangle’s size and shape.')
 add('similar-triangles','Using similar triangles','Triangles with two matching angles are similar.~The third angles must then also match.~Their corresponding side lengths have one common ratio.',
 'Mark equal angles and match corresponding sides.~Find the known scale factor.~Use it to find the missing length, even if a triangle is rotated.',
 'A small triangle has base 4 cm and height 3 cm.~A similar triangle has matching base 10 cm: scale factor 2.5.~Its matching height is 3 × 2.5 = 7.5 cm.',
 'Similar triangles have matching sides 8 cm and 20 cm. Their other matching sides are x cm and 15 cm. Find x.',6,'Large-to-small scale factor is 20 ÷ 8 = 2.5.~x × 2.5 = 15.~x = 15 ÷ 2.5 = 6 cm.',
 'The drawing’s orientation does not change which sides match.','Corresponding sides=Sides in matching positions in similar shapes.~AA similarity=Two matching triangle angles are enough to show similarity.',
 'Two triangles have matching angles but one is twice as wide. Are they congruent?','No; they are similar.','Yes; equal angles prove equal size.','No; their angles cannot match.','Equal angles fix shape but allow different sizes.')
 add('quadratic-solve','Solving factorised quadratics','A quadratic includes a squared unknown.~If a product is zero, at least one factor must be zero.~A quadratic can have two different solutions.',
 'Rearrange to make one side zero.~Factorise the quadratic.~Set each factor equal to zero and solve.',
 'x² + 5x + 6 = 0 becomes (x + 2)(x + 3) = 0.~So x + 2 = 0 or x + 3 = 0.~The solutions are x = −2 and x = −3.',
 'Solve x² − x − 12 = 0 by factorising.',None,'Find factors with product −12 and sum −1: −4 and 3.~(x − 4)(x + 3) = 0.~x = 4 or x = −3.',
 'The zero-product rule only applies when the product equals zero.','Quadratic equation=An equation whose highest power of the unknown is two.~Root of an equation=A value that makes the equation true.',
 'Solve (x − 2)(x + 5) = 0.','x = 2 or x = −5.','x = −2 or x = 5.','x = 2 only.','Set each factor to zero: x − 2 = 0 or x + 5 = 0.')
 add('algebra-extend','Identities and three brackets','An identity is true for every allowed value.~An equation may be true only for particular values.~Three brackets can be expanded two at a time.',
 'Expand the first two brackets.~Multiply every resulting term by every term in the third.~Collect like terms and check by substituting a value.',
 '(x + 1)(x + 2) = x² + 3x + 2.~Multiply by (x + 3) to get x³ + 6x² + 11x + 6.~At x = 1, both forms give 24.',
 'Expand (x + 1)(x + 1)(x + 2).',None,'The first pair gives x² + 2x + 1.~Multiply each term by x + 2.~Collect terms: x³ + 4x² + 5x + 2.',
 'Checking one value can find an error, but does not prove an identity for every value.','Identity=A statement true for every allowed value of its variables.~Cubic expression=An expression with highest power three.',
 'Which statement is an identity?','2(x + 3) ≡ 2x + 6.','x + 3 = 5.','x² = 9.','Expanding shows the first statement is true for every x.')
 add('pythagoras-check','Checking right angles and coordinate distances','The longest side is opposite the largest angle.~If its square equals the sum of the other two squares, the triangle is right-angled.~Coordinate gaps can make the two shorter sides.',
 'Identify the longest side before checking.~For a coordinate distance, find the horizontal and vertical gaps.~Use Pythagoras on that right-angled triangle.',
 'Sides 5, 12, 13 give 5² + 12² = 25 + 144 = 169 = 13².~So the triangle is right-angled.~The distance from (1,2) to (6,14) is also 13 units.',
 'Find the distance from (−1,2) to (5,10).',10,'The horizontal gap is 6 and vertical gap is 8.~Distance² = 6² + 8² = 100.~Distance = 10 units.',
 'The longest side must be the one squared on its own.','Hypotenuse=The side opposite the right angle in a right-angled triangle.~Converse=A statement that reverses the direction of an implication.',
 'Are lengths 6, 8 and 11 a right-angled triangle?','No.','Yes, because 6 + 8 > 11.','Yes, because 11 is longest.','6² + 8² = 100, but 11² = 121, so the right-angle test fails.')
 add('graph-families','Reciprocal, exponential and cubic graphs','A reciprocal graph such as y = 1/x has two separate branches.~An exponential graph such as y = 2ˣ multiplies by the same factor for each unit rise in x.~A cubic graph such as y = x³ has a different shape from a quadratic.',
 'Build a table with suitable negative, zero and positive inputs.~For 1/x, skip x = 0 and keep the branches separate.~Use the graph plotter to compare the families.',
 'For y = 2ˣ, x = −1, 0, 1, 2 gives y = 0.5, 1, 2, 4.~For y = 1/x, x = −2, −1, 1, 2 gives −0.5, −1, 1, 0.5.~For y = x³, x = −2, −1, 0, 1, 2 gives −8, −1, 0, 1, 8.',
 'Give the y-intercept of y = 2ˣ. Explain why y = 1/x has no y-intercept.',None,'At x = 0, 2⁰ = 1, so the intercept is (0,1).~For 1/x, x = 0 would divide by zero.~So it has no point on the y-axis.',
 'Do not join the two reciprocal branches across x = 0.','Exponential growth=Growth by a constant multiplier over equal input steps.~Asymptote=A line a curve gets closer to; for 1/x these are the axes.',
 'What is 2⁻¹?','1/2.','−2.','0.','A negative power means a reciprocal: 2⁻¹ = 1/2.')
 add('sets','Sets and Venn diagrams','A set is a collection of items.~A ∩ B means items in both sets.~A ∪ B means items in A, B or both.',
 'Put shared items in the overlap once.~Then fill each set’s remaining region.~The complement A′ contains items in the universal set but outside A.',
 'Out of 20 pupils, 8 like A only, 4 like both, 5 like B only and 3 like neither.~A ∩ B has 4 pupils; A ∪ B has 17.~P(A) = (8 + 4)/20 = 3/5.',
 'A Venn diagram has A only 7, both 3, B only 6 and neither 4. Find P(A ∪ B) and P(A ∩ B).',None,'The total is 20.~The union has 7 + 3 + 6 = 16, so probability 16/20 = 4/5.~The intersection has 3, so probability 3/20.',
 'The overlap belongs to the union once, not twice.','Intersection=Items belonging to both sets.~Union=Items belonging to at least one of the sets.',
 'A ∩ B refers to which Venn region?','The overlap.','Everything in either circle.','Everything outside both circles.','Intersection means membership of both A and B.')
 add('expected','Expected outcomes','An expected frequency is a prediction over many trials.~Expected frequency = probability × number of trials.~Actual results can differ because outcomes are random.',
 'Use a probability supported by the question or experiment.~Multiply by the planned number of trials.~Describe the result as an expectation, not a guarantee.',
 'A fair die has probability 1/6 of showing 4.~In 180 rolls, the expected number of 4s is 180 × 1/6 = 30.~The actual number need not be exactly 30.',
 'A spinner has probability 0.35 of red. Find the expected number of reds in 200 spins.',70,'Use probability × trials.~0.35 × 200 = 70.~This is an expected count, not a promised result.',
 'A long-term probability does not force an exact count in one experiment.','Expected frequency=Probability multiplied by the number of trials.~Random variation=Differences in results caused by chance.',
 'A coin is fair. How many heads are expected in 80 tosses?','40.','80.','Exactly 40 with no variation.','The expected count is 80 × 1/2 = 40, but the actual count can vary.')
 add('independent','Independent and mutually exclusive events','Independent events do not affect each other’s probability.~Mutually exclusive events cannot happen together.~These are different ideas.',
 'For independent events, multiply probabilities to find both happening.~For mutually exclusive alternatives, add probabilities to find either.~Check the situation before choosing a rule.',
 'Two fair coin tosses are independent.~P(head then head) = 1/2 × 1/2 = 1/4.~On one die roll, 1 and 6 are mutually exclusive, so P(1 or 6) = 2/6.',
 'A fair coin is tossed and a fair die rolled independently. Find P(head and a 6).',None,'P(head) = 1/2 and P(6) = 1/6.~Multiply for both independent events.~The probability is 1/12.',
 'Drawing without replacement can change the next probability.','Independent events=Events for which one happening does not change the other’s probability.~Mutually exclusive events=Events that cannot happen together.',
 'On a single die roll, are “a 2” and “a 5” mutually exclusive?','Yes.','No, they are independent.','Only on a fair die.','One roll cannot show both 2 and 5.')
 add('enlargement','Enlargement from a centre','An enlargement changes distances from a chosen centre by a scale factor.~Matching angles stay equal.~A fractional positive factor between 0 and 1 makes the image smaller.',
 'Draw a ray from the centre through each vertex.~Multiply every centre-to-vertex distance by the factor.~A negative factor puts the image on the opposite side of the centre.',
 'Enlarge (2,1) from (0,0) with factor 3.~Multiply both coordinates by 3.~The image is (6,3).',
 'Enlarge (4,6) from (0,0) by scale factor 1/2. Then describe the effect of factor −1.',None,'Factor 1/2 gives (2,3).~Factor −1 puts each point the same distance on the opposite side of the centre.~It gives (−4,−6) for the original point.',
 'A full description needs both centre and scale factor.','Centre of enlargement=The fixed point from which distances are scaled.~Enlargement=A transformation scaling every distance from a centre by one factor.',
 'Enlarge (3,2) from the origin by factor 2.','(6,4).','(5,4).','(1.5,1).','Both centre-to-point coordinate distances double.')
 add('rotation','Rotating about a point','A rotation turns a shape around a centre.~It preserves size and shape.~Its description needs a centre, angle and direction.',
 'Mark the centre and use tracing paper if useful.~Turn every vertex through the same angle.~Measure from the centre, not from a shape’s corner unless that is the centre.',
 '(3,1) rotated 90° anticlockwise about (0,0) becomes (−1,3).~The point stays the same distance from the origin.~For a 180° turn, (x,y) becomes (−x,−y).',
 'Rotate (2,5) 90° clockwise about the origin. Give the image and a full description.',None,'The image is (5,−2).~The angle is 90° clockwise.~The centre is (0,0).',
 'Clockwise and anticlockwise give different images for a 90° turn.','Rotation=A turn around a fixed centre.~Anticlockwise=The direction opposite to clock hands.',
 'Rotate (4,−1) 180° about (0,0).','(−4,1).','(1,4).','(4,1).','A half-turn reverses both coordinate signs.')
 add('translation','Translations and column vectors','A translation slides every point by the same amount.~It changes position but keeps size and orientation.~A column vector records horizontal movement above vertical movement.',
 'Use positive x for right and negative x for left.~Use positive y for up and negative y for down.~Add the same vector to each vertex.',
 'The vector (3, −2) means 3 right and 2 down.~It takes (−1,4) to (2,2).~Every vertex moves in the same way.',
 'Translate (−3,5) by the vector (4,−7). Find its image and describe the movement.',None,'x = −3 + 4 = 1.~y = 5 − 7 = −2.~The image is (1,−2), moving 4 right and 7 down.',
 'In a column vector, the horizontal movement is the top number.','Translation=A transformation moving every point by the same vector.~Vector=A quantity describing a directed movement.',
 'Which movement is represented by (−2,3)?','2 left and 3 up.','2 up and 3 left.','2 right and 3 down.','The first component changes x and the second changes y.')
 add('transform-series','Combining transformations','Several transformations can be applied in order.~The second transformation acts on the first image.~Reversing the order can change the final result.',
 'Write the coordinates after each step.~Use the stated centre or mirror line every time.~Check the final image rather than assuming the operations can be swapped.',
 'Start at (1,2), translate 3 right to (4,2), then reflect in the y-axis to (−4,2).~Reverse the order: reflect to (−1,2), then translate to (2,2).~The final points differ.',
 'Start at (2,1). Translate 2 up, then rotate 180° about (0,0). Find the final point.',None,'Translation gives (2,3).~A 180° rotation reverses both signs.~The final point is (−2,−3).',
 'Apply transformations in the order given.','Combined transformation=A sequence of transformations applied one after another.~Invariant=A feature or point unchanged by a transformation.',
 'Do a translation and a reflection always give the same result if their order is reversed?','No.','Yes.','Only because they preserve size.','The second step acts on a different intermediate image when the order changes.')
 add('simultaneous','Two equations at once','A simultaneous solution makes two equations true at the same time.~On a graph, it is a common intersection.~Elimination removes one unknown by adding or subtracting equations.',
 'Line up the matching terms.~Add or subtract to remove one unknown.~Find the other unknown, then substitute back.',
 'x + y = 9 and x − y = 3.~Add the equations: 2x = 12, so x = 6.~Then y = 9 − 6 = 3.',
 'Solve x + y = 11 and x − y = 5. Check both equations.',None,'Adding gives 2x = 16, so x = 8.~Then y = 3.~Checks: 8 + 3 = 11 and 8 − 3 = 5.',
 'A solution must satisfy both equations.','Simultaneous equations=Equations solved using the same values of their unknowns.~Elimination=Combining equations to remove one unknown.',
 'Does (x,y) = (4,2) solve x + y = 6 and x − y = 2?','Yes, both are true.','No, only the first is true.','No, x and y must be equal.','4 + 2 = 6 and 4 − 2 = 2.')
 add('simultaneous-adjust','Adjusting equations before elimination','Equivalent equations have the same solutions.~Multiplying every term by a non-zero number keeps an equation equivalent.~This can make coefficients match before elimination.',
 'Choose the unknown to remove.~Multiply a whole equation, including the constant.~Add or subtract, then substitute back and check.',
 '2x + y = 11 and 3x + 2y = 19.~Double the first: 4x + 2y = 22. Subtract the second: x = 3.~Then y = 5, which works in both equations.',
 'Solve 2x + y = 13 and 3x + 2y = 23.',None,'Double the first equation: 4x + 2y = 26.~Subtract the second to get x = 3.~Substitute into 2x + y = 13 to get y = 7.',
 'Multiply every term on both sides of an equation.','Equivalent equations=Equations with the same solution set.~Substitution method=Replacing one unknown using an equivalent expression from another equation.',
 'Double x + 3y = 8. What is the equivalent equation?','2x + 6y = 16.','2x + 3y = 8.','2x + 6y = 8.','Every term on both sides is multiplied by 2.')
 add('trig-angle','Finding angles with trigonometry','Sine, cosine and tangent link sides and angles in right-angled triangles.~An inverse trig function finds an angle from a ratio.~Use degree mode for angles measured in degrees.',
 'Label opposite, adjacent and hypotenuse from the chosen angle.~Choose the ratio using the two known sides.~Use sin⁻¹, cos⁻¹ or tan⁻¹, then round as requested.',
 'Opposite = 3 cm and adjacent = 4 cm.~tan θ = 3/4, so θ = tan⁻¹(0.75).~θ is about 36.9°.',
 'A right-angled triangle has opposite side 5 cm and hypotenuse 10 cm. Find the chosen acute angle.',30,'sin θ = 5/10 = 0.5.~θ = sin⁻¹(0.5) in degree mode.~θ = 30°.',
 'sin⁻¹ is inverse sine here; it is not 1/sin.','Inverse sine=The function used to find an angle from its sine ratio.~Acute angle=An angle greater than 0° and less than 90°.',
 'cos θ = 0.5 for an acute angle. What is θ?','60°.','30°.','0.5°.','Use inverse cosine in degree mode: cos⁻¹(0.5) = 60°.')
 add('trig-choice','Choosing Pythagoras or trigonometry','Pythagoras links three sides of a right-angled triangle.~Trigonometry links an angle with a side ratio.~Choose from the information given.',
 'Two known sides and one missing side: use Pythagoras.~A known acute angle and one side: choose sine, cosine or tangent.~For a 3-D problem, identify the right-angled triangle in a plane first.',
 'To find the hypotenuse from sides 5 and 12, use √(5² + 12²) = 13.~With hypotenuse 10 and an angle of 30°, the opposite side is 10 sin 30° = 5.~The methods answer different kinds of question.',
 'A ladder is 10 m long and makes a 60° angle with horizontal ground. Find its vertical height. Give a value to 2 decimal places.',8.66,'The ladder is the hypotenuse and height is opposite.~Height = 10 sin 60°.~Height is about 8.66 m.',
 'The longest labelled side in a perspective sketch is not always the hypotenuse; locate the right angle.','Trigonometric ratio=A ratio of two sides of a right-angled triangle relative to a chosen angle.~Plane=A flat two-dimensional surface.',
 'You know two shorter sides of a right-angled triangle and need its hypotenuse. Which method fits?','Pythagoras.','Sine needs to be used first.','The angles must be measured first.','Pythagoras uses the two known sides directly.')

 add('round-dp','Rounding whole numbers and decimals','Rounding chooses a nearby number at the accuracy requested.~For decimal places, count digits after the decimal point.~A number line can show the two possible rounded values.',
 'Find the two nearest allowed values.~Locate the midpoint between them.~Use the nearer value; at the midpoint, use the usual school rule to round up.',
 'To round 2.37 to one decimal place, compare 2.3 and 2.4.~Their midpoint is 2.35.~2.37 is closer to 2.4, so it rounds to 2.4.',
 'Round 6.482 to two decimal places. Explain which digit decides the rounding.',6.48,'Keep the hundredths digit, 8.~The next digit is 2, so do not round up.~The answer is 6.48.',
 'Counting decimal places begins after the decimal point, not at the first non-zero digit.','Decimal place=A digit position to the right of the decimal point.~Rounding=Replacing a number with a nearby number at a stated accuracy.',
 'What is 3.76 rounded to one decimal place?','3.8.','3.7.','4.','The hundredths digit is 6, so the tenths round up from 7 to 8.')
 add('first-quadrant','Plotting positive coordinates','Coordinates locate a point on a grid.~Write the horizontal x value first, then the vertical y value.~The first quadrant contains points with positive x and positive y.',
 'Start at the origin, where the axes cross.~Move along by x, then up by y.~Check the axis scale before counting squares.',
 'For (3,2), start at (0,0).~Move 3 units right and 2 units up.~Mark a point and label it (3,2).',
 'Describe how to plot (4,3). Then explain how (3,4) would be different.',None,'Move 4 units right from the origin.~Then move 3 units up.~(3,4) is 3 units right and 4 up, so it is a different point.',
 'The coordinate order matters: along first, then up.','Coordinate pair=Two numbers written (x,y) to locate a point.~First quadrant=The region of a coordinate grid where x and y are both positive.',
 'For (5,2), how far do you move right and up from the origin?','5 right, 2 up.','2 right, 5 up.','5 up, 2 left.','Read x first and y second: 5 along, then 2 up.')

